{"kind":"request","text":"Client note amendment: how a custody record ends when the member\ngives up. Planner work, a child of e5b86654. Found by NF-5 (6ae06f04,\nquestion b).\n\nScene: Ana's page lost every reply of a request, and the signing\nexpired. A read after the death does not hold the key. Ana presses\n\"Give up\". The record stays unresolved in custody. After 256 such\nrecords the store refuses every new request.\n\nThe note `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md` says that a `refused`\nrecord does not count in the 256 (section 5.3). It gives no end to a\nrecord whose latest signing is dead, has no refusal and is not found.\nC1 closes each earlier signing on such a read and keeps the latest\nsigning open.\n\nThe amendment states:\n\n1. The condition under which a record with no live signing and no\n   signing that can still seal becomes terminal, and the phase that\n   it takes. No new stored word if an existing phase carries the\n   meaning.\n2. Who makes the transition: `absent`, `settle`, or a call of the\n   member's dismissal.\n3. What a later seal by a slow scope clock does to that record\n   (the caveat of section 5.3).\n4. The page status of section 6 for the record, and what `dismiss`\n   and `discard` of section 8 do to it.\n5. The test: 257 given-up requests in one store, and the 257th\n   submit signs.\n\nIt lands before build step C3, and C3 builds it.","body":{"conditions":"The amended note is approved by the checker and landed on main before C3 starts its gate. It answers the five points.","target_head":"324a067991d7f1e58277bddbae04efb5ee99cb1d","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}